**Analysis of the problem:**

For a prime $p$, Wilson's theorem gives $(p-1)! \equiv -1 \pmod{p}$, so $f(p) \le p-1$ always. The three statements ask:

1. **Part (i):** Are there infinitely many primes $p$ with $f(p) = p-1$? — i.e., infinitely many primes where $n! \not\equiv -1 \pmod{p}$ for every $1 \le n \le p-2$.
2. **Part (ii):** Does $f(p)/p \to 0$ on a density-1 subset of primes?
3. **Variant:** Is $\#\{p \le x : p \text{ prime}, f(p)=p-1\} = o(x/\log x)$?

All three are tagged `@[category research open]` and are genuinely open conjectures from Erdős (Problem 1072 on erdosproblems.com). Part (iii) is explicitly attributed as a *belief* of Hardy and Subbarao, not an established theorem.

These are unsolved research-level questions in analytic/computational number theory. No proof is known for any of the three directions. I cannot produce a complete proof for open conjectures.

